WEBVTT

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:14.550
Hi, friends. Gani here. This is My Trigger Tracker.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:21.101
Sit tall in your chair. Feet flat on the floor.

00:00:21.101 --> 00:00:25.031
Hands resting on your desk. Shoulders soft.

00:00:25.031 --> 00:00:28.961
Take one slow breath with me… and let it go.

00:00:28.961 --> 00:00:38.132
Big feelings rarely arrive from nowhere. Most of the time, something comes first. A sound. A wait. A change. Something small that starts the wave.

00:00:38.132 --> 00:00:48.612
That something-first has a name. It is called a trigger. A trigger is not the wave. It is what shows up right before the wave.

00:00:48.612 --> 00:01:00.403
Triggers are information. They are not an excuse, and they are never an accusation. They are a clue your body hands you.

00:01:00.403 --> 00:01:05.644
Let me show you how to track yours.

00:01:05.644 --> 00:01:17.434
Track one: the situations. When the room gets loud. When the wait gets long. When plans change fast. When somebody corrects you in front of everyone.

00:01:17.434 --> 00:01:29.225
Notice what those have in common: they are moments, not people. A trigger is a pattern. It is never a person, and it is never a classmate's name.

00:01:29.225 --> 00:01:41.016
Track two: your body tells you first. Shoulders climb. Hands squeeze. Your stomach feels tight, or your face feels hot.

00:01:41.016 --> 00:01:51.496
Those body signals are the early part of the track. They arrive before the wave does. That is the whole point of tracking them.

00:01:51.496 --> 00:01:52.806
Track three: the head start. When you spot the pattern early, you get a moment — one breath, one strategy, or one quiet word to a grown-up — before the wave arrives.

00:01:52.806 --> 00:02:04.597
Here is the honest part. Tracking is a head start, not a promise. Sometimes you will spot the pattern and use your moment. Sometimes the wave will come anyway.

00:02:04.597 --> 00:02:16.388
When the wave comes anyway, that is not a failure. You did not track wrong. Waves are strong, and you are still learning your own weather.

00:02:16.388 --> 00:02:25.558
And your tracks stay yours. Nobody here will ask you to name a trigger out loud. Nothing goes on paper. The tracking happens inside, where only you can see it.

00:02:25.558 --> 00:02:26.868
One more thing: you never use a trigger to blame. Not yourself, and not anyone else. A clue is a clue. It just helps you get ready.

00:02:26.868 --> 00:02:36.039
A pattern. A body signal. One breath of head start.

00:02:36.039 --> 00:02:42.589
Shoulders soft. Let your body rest.

00:02:42.589 --> 00:02:47.830
Hands down. One more slow breath… and let it go.

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:54.380
Eyes open. Look around your classroom.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:03:00.931
You're ready for what's next.
